1	// Cloudflare OAuth — "Connect Cloudflare" instead of pasting an API token.
2	//
3	// Plain server-side module (NO "use server") — imported by the OAuth routes, the
4	// server actions and the DNS helpers. Never bundled to the client; no token or
5	// secret is ever logged or returned to the browser.
6	//
7	// Nxeon is a server-side Next.js app, so we are a CONFIDENTIAL client: the
8	// Authorization Code flow with a client secret (client_secret_post), PLUS PKCE
9	// (S256) because Cloudflare advertises support for it and it costs us nothing.
10	// Cloudflare supports ONLY the authorization-code grant for third-party clients
11	// (no client-credentials / implicit / device), plus refresh_token for renewals.
12	//
13	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
14	// PROTOCOL CONSTANTS — the one block to correct if Cloudflare changes anything.
15	//
16	// CONFIRMED from Cloudflare docs + their live OIDC discovery document
17	// (https://dash.cloudflare.com/.well-known/openid-configuration, fetched
18	// 2026-07-25):
19	//   * authorize  https://dash.cloudflare.com/oauth2/auth
20	//   * token      https://dash.cloudflare.com/oauth2/token
21	//   * revoke     https://dash.cloudflare.com/oauth2/revoke
22	//   * grant_types_supported includes "authorization_code" and "refresh_token"
23	//   * token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported includes client_secret_post/basic
24	//   * code_challenge_methods_supported includes "S256"
25	//   * "offline_access" is a supported scope (that's what yields a refresh token)
26	//
27	// ASSUMED (could NOT be confirmed from public docs): the exact resource scope
28	// names for zone read + DNS edit. Cloudflare documents only that "OAuth scope
29	// names correspond to Cloudflare API token permission names" and that the full
30	// list is enumerable at `GET https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/oauth/scopes`
31	// — which requires an authenticated API token, so it can't be read from here.
32	// The API-token permissions we need are named "Zone Read" and "DNS Write"
33	// (Zone category), whose documented permission keys are `#zone:read` and
34	// `#dns_records:edit`; the default below is those minus the `#`, which is also
35	// the form Cloudflare's own first-party client (wrangler) sends (`zone:read`).
36	// If Cloudflare's client registration UI shows a different form (their docs
37	// example uses a dotted style, e.g. `workers-platform.read`), DO NOT edit code:
38	// set CLOUDFLARE_OAUTH_SCOPES in the env to the exact space-separated list
39	// shown when registering the client. Candidates, in order of likelihood:
40	//   1. "zone:read dns_records:edit offline_access"   (default below)
41	//   2. "zone.read dns.edit offline_access"
42	// We request the MINIMUM needed: read the customer's zones, write DNS records.
43	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
